\description{
A row-Z-scored expression body, the feature names, and a companion table in
which every cell holds a signed fold-change plus a significance star,
decimal-aligned down each column. Contrasts are gathered into boxed,
colour-filled group bands with one boxed cell per contrast beneath them, so
the header reads as a continuous grid.

This is \code{\link[=doAnnotatedHeatmapGrid]{doAnnotatedHeatmapGrid()}} with the guesswork removed.
}
\details{
\strong{Measured, not estimated.} \code{\link[=doAnnotatedHeatmapGrid]{doAnnotatedHeatmapGrid()}} approximates string
widths as \code{nchar * fontsize * 0.62}; here they are measured with
\code{\link[=ph_measure_in]{ph_measure_in()}}, and the decimal anchor is placed so the widest integer
part and the widest fraction-plus-stars both fit inside the cell. That is
what stops \verb{-12.34***} running into its neighbour while \code{1.02} sits alone in
a needlessly wide column.

\strong{The numbers set the row height.} They are the reason this figure exists,
so \code{cell_font_min} acts as a legibility floor and the rows grow to give the
digits clearance rather than the font shrinking to fit the rows. A large gene
set therefore produces a taller page, not a denser unreadable one.

\strong{Contrast names drive the layout.} The first dot-token becomes the group
band, the remainder becomes the sub-label, and any token constant across all
contrasts is dropped so a uniform arm disappears. Stars follow the lab
cut-points \code{0.001 / 0.01 / 0.05 / 0.1} as \verb{***}, \verb{**}, \code{*}, \code{+}.
}
